{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic","id":1558228,"title":"Limits of Supernal magic","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-02-11T05:15:08-08:00","id":1558228,"title":"Limits of Supernal magic","content":"Someone was asking on Reddit about the limitations of Mages.<br /> <br /> Which got me thinking what a mage can't do - without Archmastery/Ascension and ignoring cross splatt limitations (like sure, you can't mess with a Vampire's Blood Potency) what can't a Mage do?<br /> <br /> There is the obvious, creating a soul with magic (you need to create a new human if you want a soul...), time travel back to before your own existence (birth?), entering the Supernal (safely) is creating a Lustrum.<br /> <br /> Bringing back the dead seems reasonable doable, so doesn't belong on this list (Death can do it with an indefinite spell, Time can rewrite the actual past.... Though going back to the moment of death and faking that death with Death magic then Temporal Stuttering the corpse into the present is a lot of work to minimise changes to the time stream.... Totally worth it for Lasting effects...).<br /> <br /> There is some questions on preventing Dissonance? Prime can make sleepers into sleepwalkers for a duration, but it is unclear whether this can prevent spell effects being eroded by dissonance or only delay it.<br /> <br /> Messing with Rank 6 entities and above is limited (autolosing CoW). So add killing gods to the list.<br /> <br /> And I am ignoring Imperial Practices, so sure as Mage can't create a self-altering spell. But they can cast multiple different spells over time which is Mage altering spells... or the Practice of Entities - sure you can't remove a Vampire's fear of sunlight (or is that altering the bane for all vampires?) but you can cast a shielding spell on one vampires, protecting them from sunlight (right?).<br /> <br /> Affecting an area larger than a campus/small neighbourhood is a hard limit, but you can just cast multiple spells. <br /> <br /> What am I forgetting? Small things like healing resistant damage (though you can increase the natural healing rate of the body, and I think Word of God allows this affect resistant damage)...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-11T05:57:26-08:00","id":1558229,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>orathaic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic#post1558228\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There is some questions on preventing Dissonance? Prime can make sleepers into sleepwalkers for a duration, but it is unclear whether this can prevent spell effects being eroded by dissonance or only delay it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Making Sleepers into Sleepwalkers with Stealing Fire doesn't fully prevent Dissonance \u2014 being subject to Dissonance is a quality of any obvious Supernal magic, and the only way an obvious spell is fully immune to Dissonance is if it's cast in a natural Supernal Verge.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What am I forgetting? Small things like healing resistant damage (though you can increase the natural healing rate of the body, and I think Word of God allows this affect resistant damage)... </div> </div> </div>You specifically can't accelerate the healing rate of resistant damage, as explicitly stated in the sidebar on page 223 of the corebook.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-11T07:34:27-08:00","id":1558231,"title":"","content":"Not traveling before your own birth is only a hard limit for Time alone, because you don't have a physical presence there. You could use conjunctional Mind to possess someone that lived at the time, make a body to possess with Life and so on. Its just a Bad Idea for mages. Archmages use an Imperial Practice, which is essentially a smart spell that alters how history puts itself back together to mostly preserve your original intent. But the Unmaking of Time mages use unravels the whole thing, adds your changes and then history puts itself back together by the path of least resistance, and the farther back you go, the more unintended consequences cascade.<br /> <br /> Some other hard limits are that you cannot physically enter the Astral, cannot destroy Artifacts, cannot create Eidoforms by yourself and cannot create rotes through pure spellcasting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>New experiences are the font of creativity, when seeking inspiration, break your routine.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1278855-the-agathos-kai-sophos-a-acanthus-legacy-of-strategists\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Agathos Kai Sophos</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Acanthus Legacy of strategists (Mind/Time)</span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1360568-the-szary-stra%C5%BCnik-an-obrimos-legacy-of-scholars-of-the-glyphs-of-fate\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Szary Stra\u017cnik</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Obrimos Legacy whose invisible hands guide through the Glyphs of Fate (Fate/Prime)</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","name":"KaiserAfini","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24280_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2648"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-11T07:40:52-08:00","id":1558232,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558229#post1558229\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Making Sleepers into Sleepwalkers with Stealing Fire doesn't fully prevent Dissonance \u2014 being subject to Dissonance is a quality of any obvious Supernal magic, and the only way an obvious spell is fully immune to Dissonance is if it's cast in a natural Supernal Verge.</div> </div> </div> </div>You can use magic to grant merits.<br /> <br /> Granting someone the sleepwalker meet (like with Stealing Fire) should really prevent them from causing Dissonance - as per the rules for the sleepwalker merit. <br /> <br /> Which begs the question, can Supernal magic give someone the Liar merit?<br /> <br /> Are you saying this doesn't prevent Dissonance, only prevent one particular sleeper from triggering it? <br /> <br /> Interestingly, the two other places a spell can be cast without risking paradox, do not protect against Dissonance, I didn't realise that (the Mage's own Oneiros and in a Pocket Dimension).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You specifically can't accelerate the healing rate of resistant damage, as explicitly stated in the sidebar on page 223 of the corebook. </div> </div> </div>You are right, I missed that and misremembered.<br /> <br /> So you are left with healing via time magic (that is, the body heals naturally, and the time magic accelerates the flow of time so the side-effect is healed). <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-11T07:48:32-08:00","id":1558233,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>KaiserAfini</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558231#post1558231\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Some other hard limits are that you cannot physically enter the Astral, cannot destroy Artifacts, cannot create Eidoforms by yourself and cannot create rotes through pure spellcasting.</div> </div> </div> </div>Artifacts can be destroyed mundanely, right?<br /> <br /> You can't make one with magic. <br /> <br /> Is anyone else able to enter the Astral physically? I think the Pangeans/Astral Host, Geryo, and maybe <span style=\"color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-family:Comic Sans MS\"><span style=\"font-size:8px\">Beasts</span></span></span>?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-11T09:59:05-08:00","id":1558237,"title":"","content":"I checked in the core book and <i>Signs of Sorcery</i>, nothing speaks of how to destroy them. <i>Imperial Mysteries</i> does say destroying one is something the spells can do.<br /> <br /> So in theory, yes ? In my mind, an Artifact is something that would require specific circumstances to destroy or even damage, something with gravitas.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>New experiences are the font of creativity, when seeking inspiration, break your routine.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1278855-the-agathos-kai-sophos-a-acanthus-legacy-of-strategists\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Agathos Kai Sophos</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Acanthus Legacy of strategists (Mind/Time)</span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1360568-the-szary-stra%C5%BCnik-an-obrimos-legacy-of-scholars-of-the-glyphs-of-fate\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#1155CC\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Szary Stra\u017cnik</span></span></a><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, an Obrimos Legacy whose invisible hands guide through the Glyphs of Fate (Fate/Prime)</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24280-kaiserafini","name":"KaiserAfini","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24280_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2648"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-11T13:03:55-08:00","id":1558239,"title":"","content":"Annihilate matter spells says<br /> &quot;Magical objects and materials, such as tass or Artifacts, cannot normally be destroyed with this spell.<br /> +1 Reach: For one point of Mana, the spell can destroy magical objects as well. &quot;<br /> So i think that it s possible to destroy Artifacts","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24346-neos01","name":"Neos01","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24346_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1021"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-11T14:33:41-08:00","id":1558241,"title":"","content":"1e core lists a bunch of Artifacts with their durabikity and structure, which seems to suggest you can destroy them. It doesn't mention them being indestructable, I think that is fanon.<br /> <br /> The merit gives them the Unbreakable trait, which is Durability two higher than normal for their materials... So they are harder to destroy mundanely.<br /> <br /> There is no such reference in 2e.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-11T15:30:50-08:00","id":1558243,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>orathaic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558232#post1558232\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Are you saying this doesn't prevent Dissonance, only prevent one particular sleeper from triggering it?</div> </div> </div> </div>Dissonance is a quality of spells and Sleepers are the vector for aggravating it \u2014 taking torches away from individual people doesn't make a paperback book less flammable.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So you are left with healing via time magic (that is, the body heals naturally, and the time magic accelerates the flow of time so the side-effect is healed). </div> </div> </div>The closest to valid I can consider this is for spells that are actually displacing the character from normal interaction with the world, like Temporal Pocket; simply using time to speed up your body seems like directly cheating when Veil of Moments has all the caveats it has for the opposite situation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>orathaic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558241#post1558241\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1e core lists a bunch of Artifacts with their durabikity and structure, which seems to suggest you can destroy them. It doesn't mention them being indestructable, I think that is fanon.</div> </div> </div> </div>More concretely, they probably wouldn't include &quot;without going through the weird and difficult means of destroying them described in legends, Astras cannot be so much as scratched&quot; as a quality of a subset of Artifacts if Artifacts as a whole were indestructible \u2014 particularly since the counterpart of Astras in Changeling 1e (Prop Manifestations) were explicitly unable to be destroyed except through their singular weaknesses.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-12T01:25:33-08:00","id":1558256,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558243#post1558243\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The closest to valid I can consider this is for spells that are actually displacing the character from normal interaction with the world, like Temporal Pocket; simply using time to speed up your body seems like directly cheating when Veil of Moments has all the caveats it has for the opposite situation.</div> </div> </div> </div>Temporal Pocket seems like it should definitely work. The subject experiences that time compressed down to an instant, and it explicitly says they bleed out/heal as they normally would.<br /> <br /> But that just demonstrates Time allowing healing at a faster rate, not by actually altering the body, but by increasing the flow of time.<br /> <br /> Also take Rend Lifespan/Weight of Years, they allow rapidly aging to deal damage with less than Mastery. So it seems entirely appropriate that a spell like Accelerate be used for a healing effect (rather than the direct combat effects the spell is written for).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-12T02:49:00-08:00","id":1558258,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Neos01</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558239#post1558239\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Annihilate matter spells says<br /> &quot;Magical objects and materials, such as tass or Artifacts, cannot normally be destroyed with this spell.<br /> +1 Reach: For one point of Mana, the spell can destroy magical objects as well. &quot;<br /> So i think that it s possible to destroy Artifacts</div> </div> </div> </div>What is interesting is the Mastery of Prime can also destroy magical items (Unmaking them as Word of Unmaking describes). But it explicitly doesn't work in Artifacts...<br /> <br /> Annihilate Matter seems to work fine.<br /> <br /> I think there is some interesting question of partially taking an Artifact apart, but in a repairable way. Like when would the Artifact's power stop working and would it start again by repairing it. Could definitely shape some plot around a missing piece of a larger Artifact.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-12T15:31:48-08:00","id":1558265,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>orathaic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558233#post1558233\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is anyone else able to enter the Astral physically? I think the Pangeans/Astral Host, Geryo, and maybe <span style=\"color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-family:Comic Sans MS\"><span style=\"font-size:8px\">Beasts</span></span></span>?</div> </div> </div> </div>Demons can learn the Rip the Gates Exploit, opening a temporary physical iris into the Astral Realms through which anyone can enter bodily.<br /> <br /> Beasts can invite or pull others bodily from material realms into their Lair in the Primordial Dream, a unique borderland in the Astral Realms, from which they could then venture out into the Temenos or the Anima Mundi.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-12T16:53:32-08:00","id":1558272,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>orathaic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic#post1558228\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Bringing back the dead seems reasonable doable, so doesn't belong on this list (Death can do it with an indefinite spell, Time can rewrite the actual past.... Though going back to the moment of death and faking that death with Death magic then Temporal Stuttering the corpse into the present is a lot of work to minimise changes to the time stream.... Totally worth it for Lasting effects...).</div> </div> </div> </div>Back in first edition of Requiem, there was a character for the New Orleans setting, who is reputed to have been brought back from Final Death with the aid of necromancy.<br /> <br /> Time Magic is quite possibly the most dangerous of all the Arcana to use in any given situation. You aren't just changing one minor event in the past, you're also changing every event that comes after it. That's the premise of the butterfly effect, or chaos theory, where even the tiniest action can have far reaching consequences down the line.<br /> <br /> Using Time to accelerate the healing process is certainly doable, though I would rule that doing so shortens your life span every time you use it. There's a finite amount of cells the body can produce over the course of its life time, and a spell like that is causing you to burn out faster.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-12T17:25:35-08:00","id":1558274,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>orathaic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558256#post1558256\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Temporal Pocket seems like it should definitely work. The subject experiences that time compressed down to an instant, and it explicitly says they bleed out/heal as they normally would.<br /> <br /> But that just demonstrates Time allowing healing at a faster rate, not by actually altering the body, but by increasing the flow of time.<br /> <br /> Also take Rend Lifespan/Weight of Years, they allow rapidly aging to deal damage with less than Mastery. So it seems entirely appropriate that a spell like Accelerate be used for a healing effect (rather than the direct combat effects the spell is written for).</div> </div> </div> </div>Temporal Pocket is the specific instance I am saying works, because the spell prevents you from altering the rest of the world while you're doing so. Spells like Veil of Moments and Acceleration are doing fiddlier things to the interface between the character and the world and the sidebar I mentioned <i>specifically says </i>resistant damage cannot have its healing accelerated by magic \u2014 Temporal Pocket skirts this by simply putting the rest of the world in time-out while you subjectively experience a span of extra time that takes sand out of your personal hourglass and starkly limits what else you can do while it's active.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stupid Loserman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558265#post1558265\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Beasts can invite or pull others bodily from material realms into their Lair in the Primordial Dream, a unique borderland in the Astral Realms, from which they could then venture out into the Temenos or the Anima Mundi.</div> </div> </div> </div>Minor caveat that, while the Beast Player's Guide isn't wholly unambiguously worded on this subject, the combined notes on how Beasts leaving their Lair to wander the Astral work and the effects of the Mother's Land on travelers suggest that going from a Lair to elsewhere in the Astral without using a Primordial Pathway results in the traveler assuming a Dream Form, &quot;waking up&quot; back in the Lair if their Dream Health is depleted.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","user":"Satchel","date":".  Fixed missing prefix in a word. 02-13-2026, 08:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:17:29-08:00","id":1558276,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558274#post1558274\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Temporal Pocket is the specific instance I am saying works, because the spell prevents you from altering the rest of the world while you're doing so. Spells like Veil of Moments and Acceleration are doing fiddlier things to the interface between the character and the world and the sidebar I mentioned <i>specifically says </i>resistant damage cannot have its healing accelerated by magic \u2014 Temporal Pocket skirts this by simply putting the rest of the world in time-out while you subjectively experience a span of extra time that takes sand out of your personal hourglass and starkly limits what else you can do while it's active.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think what I'm saying is that I explicitly disagree with this interpretation.<br /> <br /> But I've already said most of what I intended above, so to a loud repeating myself I will just try to address things above.<br /> <br /> Temporal Pocket doesn't have to prevent you interacting with anything, just with anything outside the pocket. So an AoE Temporal Pocket would allow you interact with your immediate surroundings. Eat food, breath air (yes this spell is weird if you can't touch things because air is a things) and perform Oblations (useful if you have a Hallow in the pocket/legacy Oblations, though I assume a Hallow will not fill up with mana if it is trapped Inna Temporal Pocket).<br /> <br /> The Sidebar mentions accelerated healing. And that clear reference to Life ruling effects which double healing time is not disputed. Temporal Pocket also accelerated healing, in a sense but we both agree that it doesn't apply to this Time effect - it accelerates all Time effects to instant, you can starve, dehydrate, bleed out etc when in a Temporal Pocket, you can also apply first aid to a cabal mate, provide non-magical medical care, and rest.<br /> <br /> It isn't that complicated compared to Veil of Moments, which prevents bleeding out, healing, etc. (Unless you take a reach effect to exclude them). Time just seems to have a side effect of being a part of natural healing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:18:43-08:00","id":1558277,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558272#post1558272\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Time Magic is quite possibly the most dangerous of all the Arcana to use in any given situation. You aren't just changing one minor event in the past, you're also changing every event that comes after it. That's the premise of the butterfly effect, or chaos theory, where even the tiniest action can have far reaching consequences down the line.</div> </div> </div> </div>I never said anything about it bring a good idea... <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Using Time to accelerate the healing process is certainly doable, though I would rule that doing so shortens your life span every time you use it. There's a finite amount of cells the body can produce over the course of its life time, and a spell like that is causing you to burn out faster. </div> </div> </div>Shortened Lifespan seems perfectly reasonable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-13T06:20:03-08:00","id":1558282,"title":"","content":"The Time Arcanum calls out that the Present is robust against grandfather paradoxes. It is probably robust against the butterfly effect, and similar.<br /> <br /> The premise of hubris as a theme allows for a capacity of unintended consequences, but within reason (and largely deriving from things done on purpose). It's not meant to be stupid about it, not license for Storytellers to make a cute game of screwing over players, and certainly not meant to discourage some of the most basic applications of the Arcanum.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-13T13:27:21-08:00","id":1558292,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>orathaic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558276#post1558276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think what I'm saying is that I explicitly disagree with this interpretation.<br /> <br /> But I've already said most of what I intended above, so to a loud repeating myself I will just try to address things above.</div> </div> </div> </div>Because I feel like I'm being misunderstood:<br /> <br /> My interpretation is that Temporal Pocket, which causes its subject to experience time normally while cut off from most interaction with the world outside themselves due to the outside world effectively being frozen from the inside perspective, is fine as a workaround to healing resistant damage because the character is not actually healing any faster in narrative time \u2014 it would be more disruptive for a subject's resistant damage to not heal at all while under the effects of Temporal Pocket than for it to heal normally and just <i>appear</i> to be healing faster to those witnessing a before-and-after from the outside.<br /> <br /> The same cannot be said for using Time to simply <i>make the injuries heal faster</i> without changing the character's frame of reference entirely, which, Life effect or not, is against both the letter and the spirit of the sidebar. You can't time-heals-all-wounds a character who can still interact with the rest of the world out of having damage that's resistant to magically-accelerated healing when most magically accelerated healing is either directly healing damage or changing the rate at which they heal damage as an individual.<br /> <br /> Spending three hours traveling in the Hedge or a temporally-weird patch of the Shadow and coming out a hour later from the material frame of reference is different from speeding up a character's healing process to that of a werewolf's regeneration. An environmental effect or other functional and narrative movement of a character into a frame of reference that can be followed at the same subjective rate a la &quot;year inside, hour outside&quot; is mechanically and thematically different from &quot;the character heals faster&quot; \u2014 if you were to give a character with the Arcadian Metabolism Merit some amount of resistant damage, they would heal that damage slower than usual in the Hedge because the Merit's effects specifically reduce their healing time, but if that same character spent two days in the Hedge healing a box of resistant lethal damage and they come out of the Hedge an hour after they went in because of the place's metaphysical quirks, they haven't healed that damage in an hour, they were benefiting from the fact that they could spend two days in the Hedge and return to find only an hour had passed outside.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-13T14:25:14-08:00","id":1558294,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558282#post1558282\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not meant to be stupid about it, not license for Storytellers to make a cute game of screwing over players, and certainly not meant to discourage some of the most basic applications of the Arcanum.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I was lead to believe the opposite. Nearly every time I've heard people talk about the consequences of Paradox, they always jump straight to your character just instantly dying in a magical explosion, or getting banished to a parallel reality, or some other form of character deletion.<br /> <br /> I think second edition of Awakening toned this down, by giving you the option to transfer Paradox to your surrounding environment, but I'm not clear on the specifics of how that works.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-13T15:13:28-08:00","id":1558296,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558294#post1558294\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was lead to believe the opposite. Nearly every time I've heard people talk about the consequences of Paradox, they always jump straight to your character just instantly dying in a magical explosion, or getting banished to a parallel reality, or some other form of character deletion.<br /> <br /> I think second edition of Awakening toned this down, by giving you the option to transfer Paradox to your surrounding environment, but I'm not clear on the specifics of how that works.</div> </div> </div> </div>That sounds more like what little I've heard of people describing Paradox in Ascension; Awakening 1e had a strict ladder of Paradox effect types based on the number of rolled successes with containment being an elective reduction of Paradox successes, with the main wrinkle being that the least severe type of Paradox is the one that alters the spell's target and potentially inverts its effects.<br /> <br /> Adjusting for wordcount, pretty much all of 2e's Paradox Reach effects correlate to one or more of the five types of Paradox effect from 1e, whether it be creating a supernatural environmental hazard (Anomalies), summoning Abyssal beings (Manifestations), altering aspects of the mage's Pattern (Bedlam and Branding), or simply warping the spell into something different than the mage's intent (Havoc); the main difference is that 1e's advanced Paradox effects scaled with the Arcanum rating of the spell used (applying more severe derangements or overt bodily transformations or summoning more powerful Abyssal beings) while 2e offloads that to the addition of Reach as a source of Paradox dice.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-13T17:50:31-08:00","id":1558300,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558294#post1558294\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I was lead to believe the opposite. Nearly every time I've heard people talk about the consequences of Paradox, they always jump straight to your character just instantly dying in a magical explosion, or getting banished to a parallel reality, or some other form of character deletion.<br /> <br /> I think second edition of Awakening toned this down, by giving you the option to transfer Paradox to your surrounding environment, but I'm not clear on the specifics of how that works.</div> </div> </div> </div>In addition to what Satchel said, I was not referring to the mechanic of Paradox. I was referring to the idea of temporal paradoxes or similar consequences as a result of time travel, or even lesser applications of the Time Arcanum.<br /> <br /> It's actually funny to me that the First Edition book Imperial Mysteries introduced an idea of outright timeline editing as an example of the Practice of Dynamics, because I think per Second Edition it would actually be the opposite. First Edition said &quot;you need Dynamics because the spell has to be capable of making all of the micro-adjustments that you wouldn't have known about to bring about the desired change&quot;, whereas I think that in Second Edition it would more go &quot;you need Dynamics to ensure that a single change has a ripple effect of unintended and far-reaching consequences&quot;.<br /> <br /> This is because my read on what the opening premises of the Time Arcanum are mean that, with the fact that you're always travelling into a past from the frame of reference of an established present, most changes you make will only have the ripples of what is directly affected by that change. It's like, if using Divination while in the past gives you a certainty of outcomes up until the point of the present that is not shared by looking at the real future, then I'd say those things mostly hold fast. If you make a surgeon woefully incompetent the day before a specific surgery such that the most obvious consequences are that the patient died and the surgeon was fired, then those changes will crystallize. But every other person the surgeon saved thereafter? Those survivals have been established as a matter of fact. The timeline will adjust to provide somebody else to have saved them (probably with the greatest subtlety possible).<br /> <br /> Hence, you can go into the past to destroy some enemy and not return to the present with shenanigans of &quot;oh no, your removal of them from a complex chain of events means that nobody ever developed a certain cancer drug, and huge numbers of other people are also dead as a result&quot;.<br /> <br /> It's also something I would have intertwine with the necessary complexity of major world events that makes it generally impossible to stop them with single discrete actions. The usual time travel resolution of &quot;World War II happened for way more reasons than the decisions of Hitler&quot; combined with a cosmology of causality and time travel that is going to bias towards retaining World War II as an established feature of the past.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-13T18:01:26-08:00","id":1558302,"title":"","content":"Anyway, for my own contribution to the thread's premise that is more sophisticated than &quot;Supernal magic is limited by the technical parameters of spell factors&quot;:<br /> <br /> I think one thing that is consistent with spells is that the definition of the Practices and the general premise of the Supernal mean that its spells tend towards specific, discrete and direct effects, rather than stuff that is more fanciful or multi-layered. There are Supernal spells that can effect esoteric and metaphysical things, like the soul, but even then the resulting outcomes tend to be singular, rather than things with more ritual qualities.<br /> <br /> I think this contrasts with the base powers, and especially the alternate forms of ritual magic, used by other supernatural beings. I often make the mental contrast between a work of Theban Sorcery to cause beetles to emit from the mouths of liars. It's a pretty elementary piece of their magic in the interests of making a statement, but for Awakened power combines a number of effects of varying power levels. It's not just that it's not a straightforward thing to replicate, it's that the act of combining the multiple necessary actions to do so illustrates how the sum of those parts is not really an expression of Supernal power.<br /> <br /> I see this not just in terms of a mechanical necessity and means to facilitate improvised casting and creative thaumaturgy, but something that lines up with the premise of the Supernal as a state of essential forms. If the Supernal is the state where the true triangle exists, spells are the work of making real, imperfect triangles more like it. That essentialism doesn't lend itself so well to giving things the properties of a complex representative image.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-13T19:26:07-08:00","id":1558306,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558300#post1558300\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This is because my read on what the opening premises of the Time Arcanum are mean that, with the fact that you're always travelling into a past from the frame of reference of an established present, most changes you make will only have the ripples of what is directly affected by that change. It's like, if using Divination while in the past gives you a certainty of outcomes up until the point of the present that is not shared by looking at the real future, then I'd say those things mostly hold fast. If you make a surgeon woefully incompetent the day before a specific surgery such that the most obvious consequences are that the patient died and the surgeon was fired, then those changes will crystallize. But every other person the surgeon saved thereafter? Those survivals have been established as a matter of fact. The timeline will adjust to provide somebody else to have saved them (probably with the greatest subtlety possible).<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>That sounds similar to how fate works in the Vampire's Assistant novels, where destined events can be altered, but not avoided.<br /> <br /> For example, if you were to kill 'the chosen one' spoken of in a prophecy, the universe would simply appoint a new chosen one to replace them.<br /> <br /> The actors involved are changed, but the destiny still comes to pass.<br /> <br /> <br /> -------------------------------<br /> <br /> As for the subject of predicting the future: due to the existence of free will, I tend to take the Steven Universe Future Vision approach to handling this. Which is to say that the key to predicting the future is not as simple as just looking at a predetermined set of events. You must also choose to follow those events in order to bring about the outcome in your vision.<br /> <br /> As such, predicting the future turns into a game of multiple choice scenarios, where you view the different options available, and then choose the one that best suits your desired result.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-13T23:00:53-08:00","id":1558310,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558300#post1558300\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This is because my read on what the opening premises of the Time Arcanum are mean that, with the fact that you're always travelling into a past from the frame of reference of an established present, most changes you make will only have the ripples of what is directly affected by that change. <br /> <br /> &lt;Snip&gt;<br /> The usual time travel resolution of &quot;World War II happened for way more reasons than the decisions of Hitler&quot; combined with a cosmology of causality and time travel that is going to bias towards retaining World War II as an established feature of the past.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I think I'm more agnostic about it.<br /> <br /> Re-reading the intro to the Time Arcanum, and I can't see 2e explicitly supporting this interpretation. It does describe the Past as a spins thread which is hard to alter, while the future is far more unpredictable.<br /> <br /> But when you actually go back in time it is unclear whether the threads will be weaving back in the same way. I think I would let a game dictate how much things change. But generally, having historic events remain similar (like the Great Depression still happens even if you change who became President in the US, and the resulting rise of fascism in Europe still happens - even if the details are different... Maybe the Spanish civil war goes differently and Franco rather than Hitler starts World War 2... ). <br /> <br /> But the more minute things seem like they should be easy to change. A surgeon doing/losing their job isn't going to have no impact on their patients. It may have hundreds of little changes.... I'm just not sure what a game would want to do with them in a narrative sense. You want your players to have some control over the narrative, some idea what the consequences of their actions will be. So having wild unpredictable changes may be less desirable from a gameplay perspective (never mind the difficulty for the ST of working out how those changes should impact the present).<br /> <br /> Games should be fun, and interactions meaningful. If you intend small shifts in the past to result in massive shifts in the present, then you should inform your players of the fact up front. So at least they know what to expect. But (while I don't know this is the most fun option) I can't see the text explicitly saying this chaotic result isn't how time travel should be viewed - I don't think the text is explicit either way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-13T23:22:07-08:00","id":1558312,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558302#post1558302\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anyway, for my own contribution to the thread's premise that is more sophisticated than &quot;Supernal magic is limited by the technical parameters of spell factors&quot;:<br /> <br /> I think one thing that is consistent with spells is that the definition of the Practices and the general premise of the Supernal mean that its spells tend towards specific, discrete and direct effects, rather than stuff that is more fanciful or multi-layered. There are Supernal spells that can effect esoteric and metaphysical things, like the soul, but even then the resulting outcomes tend to be singular, rather than things with more ritual qualities.<br /> <br /> I think this contrasts with the base powers, and especially the alternate forms of ritual magic, used by other supernatural beings. I often make the mental contrast between a work of Theban Sorcery to cause beetles to emit from the mouths of liars. It's a pretty elementary piece of their magic in the interests of making a statement, but for Awakened power combines a number of effects of varying power levels. It's not just that it's not a straightforward thing to replicate, it's that the act of combining the multiple necessary actions to do so illustrates how the sum of those parts is not really an expression of Supernal power.<br /> <br /> I see this not just in terms of a mechanical necessity and means to facilitate improvised casting and creative thaumaturgy, but something that lines up with the premise of the Supernal as a state of essential forms. If the Supernal is the state where the true triangle exists, spells are the work of making real, imperfect triangles more like it. That essentialism doesn't lend itself so well to giving things the properties of a complex representative image.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you for that contribution. I thi I it is rather insightful.<br /> <br /> I would note that (at least in my understanding) Mages are often limited by reality (in a way that, say, Demons may not be), a Compelling spell can only cause things which are already possible. Channeling mana and essence aware different practices because the different forms of energy act differently in their natural forms.<br /> <br /> So Theban sourcery being part of the natural way the world works makes certain things easier for a Mage (if she studies Theban sourcery, at least - which is probably not that easy). Replicating the effect may be more akin to Sons of Phobos style mundane recreation that actual Supernal Magic, and that is fine. But how a sourcerous working looks to Focused Mage sight or is described by Supernal Symbols is an interesting quirk.<br /> <br /> I would ask whether Demon exploits are the same as magic (from a supernal perspective) - like can Mages actually *not* study them, given the sort of metaphysics described (the metaphor I'm thinking of it how Demons alter the hardware while Mages are studying the source code... You can't even see what a Demon is doing if you just look at the code...).<br /> <br /> And I know I said I wouldn't be thinking about other Splatts, because that way madness lies. But &quot;how things work&quot; as described by the Supernal seems to be the Magey part.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-14T14:41:22-08:00","id":1558327,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>orathaic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558312#post1558312\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So Theban sourcery being part of the natural way the world works makes certain things easier for a Mage (if she studies Theban sourcery, at least - which is probably not that easy).</div> </div> </div> </div>This is one of those cases where I think the Mage corebook's description of the wider mysteries of the world in terms of &quot;there are influences upon the Fallen World that are extradimensional and may even have a capacity to ascend into the Supernal of their own accord&quot; has value.<br /> <br /> So Theban Sorcery might not be &quot;part of the natural world&quot; so much as &quot;what the guiding energy of life looks like after being focused through the lens of whatever Lower Depth the Strix came from&quot; with that touch of &quot;the thematic quality of vampiric nature that is a course from the Divine that may or may not exist entirely in the minds of vampires&quot;.<br /> <br /> Either way, a thing that kind of accumulates a complexity or a profaned origin that does not readily translate to Supernal magic no matter how much you study it (even if studying it provides more insight into the Supernal by way of analysing the absence or the contrast).<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-14T16:02:07-08:00","id":1558331,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Isator Levi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558327#post1558327\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Either way, a thing that kind of accumulates a complexity or a profaned origin that does not readily translate to Supernal magic no matter how much you study it (even if studying it provides more insight into the Supernal by way of analysing the absence or the contrast).</div> </div> </div> </div>I should be clearer, when I say &quot;Sons of Phobos&quot; style, I don't necessarily mean Supernal magic exceeding the limits you described, I mean - look the Sons of Phobos are humans doing blood magic, you don't need to be undead (just maybe eat some Vampire's heart of something...).<br /> <br /> Mages are just the spatt to have the straight up curiosity (addicted to mysteries) and the powers (Mage sight) to figure shit out - which if mortal humans can do it, mages with the ability to look back in time or prophecy where you are planning to be, sure as hell can figure out how to investigate that thing which caught their attention...<br /> <br /> Add to that the potential to generate their own Vitae with supernal magic, or take other short cuts not available to mortals. Even if it involved using sleepwalkers as experiments... Yeah, the Sons of Phobos, or Alchemist in Promethean, do some pretty amazing things, which Supernal magic likely can't directly minic. And the only downside of being Awakened is being blind to doing things any other way... Like why carry a gun when you can literally tear someone's pattern apart with a spell, is the kind of \u00bfLazy? thinking which should afflict a mage. Why do things the hard way when Magic, really doesn't lend them towards trying Alchemy/Blood Magic or similar.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":27248,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","user":"orathaic","date":". 02-15-2026, 04:13 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-14T17:38:11-08:00","id":1558335,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>orathaic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558312#post1558312\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would ask whether Demon exploits are the same as magic (from a supernal perspective) - like can Mages actually *not* study them, given the sort of metaphysics described (the metaphor I'm thinking of it how Demons alter the hardware while Mages are studying the source code... You can't even see what a Demon is doing if you just look at the code...).</div> </div> </div> </div>The analogy I usually make is that if reality is a video game, demons are speedrunners, the God-Machine is a tool-assisted speedrun, and mages are Cheat Engine. Demons have the ingenuity, expertise, and reflexes necessary to recognize and utilize glitches, sometimes very complicated ones: things anyone could <i>hypothetically</i> do to yield unexpected results, but most people don't know how to, and which require practiced reflexes to execute successfully. The God-Machine is doing the same thing on a greater scale. Its vast intelligence and calculation network is able to prepare very complicated, far-thought-out interactions, keeping all of the complicated glitch inputs in a very long input sequence memorized and frame-perfect. Mages do not need these complicated occult interactions. They are instead able to see into the &quot;code&quot; of supernal associations and directly alter select values in real time.<br /> <br /> The analogy is not perfect. What makes demons able to execute Embeds is not <i>just</i> ingenuity and reflexes, but the subtle perceptions and workings of the multifoliate demonic mind, which is both physical and metaphysical at once. (This is a trait certain humans who express demon-blooded parentage share enough that they <i>can</i> learn and use Embeds.) Mages have insight into the supernal girding under the universe, but they do not have multifoliate metabrains which can engage in extradimensional thinking. Whether a mage could reproduce a multifoliate metabrain through supernal sorcery (and what kind of awful wreckage they would leave behind in their failed experiments before perfecting the process) is an open question for a chronicle to explore.<br /> <br /> Exploits don't just employ this fluid extradimensional finesse, but use it to direct the flow of Aether in patterns to accomplish their causality-distorting results. If you want to strain the analogy to its breaking point, there is a speedrun technique for Dragon Quest that literally involves temporarily cooking the cartridge on a hot plate, making the game's memory more volatile in order to make a particular glitch more likely to execute. Call it controlled memory damage. A mage trying to reproduce an Exploit (as opposed to reproducing its results through other means) would definitely have to access a source of Aether, successfully manipulate it, and quite likely still need that extradimensional finesse to control that burst of Aether correctly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-14T20:29:05-08:00","id":1558337,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Stupid Loserman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1558228-limits-of-supernal-magic?p=1558335#post1558335\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The analogy is not perfect. What makes demons able to execute Embeds is not <i>just</i> ingenuity and reflexes, but the subtle perceptions and workings of the multifoliate demonic mind, which is both physical and metaphysical at once. (This is a trait certain humans who express demon-blooded parentage share enough that they <i>can</i> learn and use Embeds.) Mages have insight into the supernal girding under the universe, but they do not have multifoliate metabrains which can engage in extradimensional thinking. Whether a mage could reproduce a multifoliate metabrain through supernal sorcery (and what kind of awful wreckage they would leave behind in their failed experiments before perfecting the process) is an open question for a chronicle to explore.</div> </div> </div> </div>To take a different tack that also draws on what we know about demon-blooded characters, Demon's metaphysic often deals with lower-case versions of things Mage assigns Capital Letters to \u2014 the Awakened metaphysic trucks with Forms and Instruments where the Unchained deal with forms and instruments, and where the Awakened become what they are through a confrontation with the Lie, demon-blooded characters who don't have their Embeds from birth often develop them when they realize that <i>the universe is lying.</i><br /> <br /> Embeds are variously naturally-occurring or deliberately-engineered and maintained exceptions to common natural law that the God-Machine makes use of, which, taken as part of the occult physics that make Infrastructure work, are functionally in the same vein as Ceremonies and sorcerous Rites; as the God-Machine is the sum total of all its work and the Unchained are largeish pieces broken off of that structure, they have certain advantages in making use of smaller subroutines they manage to remember or get the hang of, while their demon-blooded offspring develop facility with more Embeds and Interlocks as they draw the attention of the God-Machine (in the reverse of order their parents do those things, and getting Embeds that aren't given to them for free in this fashion is more difficult than it is for demons).<br /> <br /> Derived as they are from the toolkit of an architect dwelling in the structure of structures, demons are essentially self-directed complex ritual actors unmoored from their original purposes, capable of incorporating the smaller ritual effects of Embeds into their metaphorical bodies and moving around metaphysical loose ends to make greater deviations from the laws of common reality; demon-blooded instead approach this aggregation of lore at their peril and have less ability to work outside the system's margin of error due to never having broken the leash to begin with; to bring it back to Mage, becoming stigmatic like all active demon-blooded are prevents you from Awakening at the same time as it permanently enmeshes you with the metaphysical framework of the Machine (and Latents becoming mechanically identical to Offspring in any circumstance that would normally cause them to become stigmatics blurs the line with regular stigmatics further).<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Awakening clears external supernatural conditions of the sort that might steer a character toward integration with the Machine on terms not their own and puts you in a state where magical effects at your disposal are mostly created from first principles instead of tapping into an existing framework, but studying such frameworks gives you more insight into the fundamentals your own magic is drawn from. Mages can absolutely study Exploits \u2014 they can be the source of a Mystery like any other supernatural phenomenon \u2014 but spells don't work like Embeds do and making effects that operate in such a fashion instead of stringing together combined spells that emulate the end result is the work of archmagic, and the principle of using Aether to turbocharge Embeds into Exploits is adjacent to Enraptured magical savant abilities at best and Paradox at worst; dipping into a volatile well of power from the strains in reality around great magical workings is hard to thematically emulate in a splat whose core ability is already yanking localized ad-hoc rules for reality out of the raw chaos of creation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}